WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.660
The exact additive model predicts minus
seventy point six eight at the quiet

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.350
Monday hour, whose target is five.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.970
Its absolute error is about
seventy five point six eight.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.370
Applying maximum of zero and
prediction gives zero, with error five.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.530
We retain both values as separate outputs.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.570
At the busy hour,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.450
the positive forecast near one
hundred and seventy five is unchanged,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.150
so the large deficit against
four hundred and twenty remains.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.660
This is an explicit prediction policy,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:37.170
rather than a display adjustment that
secretly changes what was evaluated.

00:00:39.358 --> 00:00:42.248
The raw branch returns a
copy of the predictions.

00:00:42.918 --> 00:00:46.928
The nonnegative branch applies
maximum with zero to every row.

00:00:47.458 --> 00:00:51.228
No rounding is introduced,
and the supplied array is not mutated.

00:00:52.078 --> 00:00:56.718
Each policy has separate aligned
prediction files and scores on the same

00:00:56.718 --> 00:00:57.468
identities.

00:00:58.138 --> 00:01:01.838
Applying a different policy only to a
chart would make that chart inconsistent

00:01:01.838 --> 00:01:03.508
with its saved metrics.

00:01:04.378 --> 00:01:09.068
The eventual serving function must use
the frozen model and policy together,

00:01:09.218 --> 00:01:12.128
although the application
interface remains later work.

00:01:14.275 --> 00:01:19.005
When the prediction is below zero
and the target is at least zero,

00:01:19.375 --> 00:01:22.525
raw absolute error is
target minus prediction.

00:01:23.175 --> 00:01:26.005
Clipping leaves error
equal to the target alone,

00:01:26.395 --> 00:01:29.225
reducing it by the magnitude
of the negative prediction.

00:01:30.175 --> 00:01:34.085
Squaring preserves that ordering
because both errors are nonnegative.

00:01:34.755 --> 00:01:38.405
For predictions already above zero,
neither error changes.

00:01:39.215 --> 00:01:43.845
The claim concerns absolute and squared
error against nonnegative targets.

00:01:44.395 --> 00:01:48.505
It is not a guarantee about every
possible metric or demand quantity.

00:01:49.235 --> 00:01:53.135
Clipping fixes an output constraint
without teaching the missing busy hour

00:01:53.135 --> 00:01:53.725
shape.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:00.740
The exact one hot model has seven hundred
and twenty four negative validation

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:01.590
forecasts.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.610
Clipping reduces its absolute error to
about one hundred and nine point one four.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.508
The interaction model has seven hundred
and fifty one negatives and reaches one

00:02:11.508 --> 00:02:13.490
hundred point five two after clipping.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.470
This can reverse a close ranking
between penalties or encodings,

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.930
so the policy must be explicit
in the comparison table.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.130
Both are still worse than the
hour and working day baseline.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.000
Retaining all rows lets us separate the
constraint correction from the remaining

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.690
positive forecast errors.

00:02:34.942 --> 00:02:36.402
Every scaler,

00:02:36.402 --> 00:02:41.112
pilot rate and model is fitted
before validation is loaded.

00:02:41.522 --> 00:02:45.832
Validation is then deliberately used
to choose a development reference.

00:02:46.522 --> 00:02:49.952
We keep all twenty eight
model and policy combinations,

00:02:50.442 --> 00:02:52.612
including both existing baselines.

00:02:53.142 --> 00:02:56.352
The rule minimizes
validation absolute error;

00:02:56.982 --> 00:03:01.932
exact ties prefer raw policy and
then deterministic model name order.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:05.472
Groups wins at ninety
one point five seven.

00:03:05.922 --> 00:03:11.432
Its outputs are already nonnegative,
so both policies tie and raw is retained.

00:03:12.162 --> 00:03:14.492
The selection file saves the model hash,

00:03:14.742 --> 00:03:17.992
policy and protocol identity
for the next comparison.

00:03:20.442 --> 00:03:23.532
This is a frozen reference
for further development,

00:03:23.882 --> 00:03:28.012
not an externally validated
winner or a completed application.

00:03:28.682 --> 00:03:31.832
The same validation window has
informed several comparisons,

00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:34.892
so its selected score can be optimistic.

00:03:35.482 --> 00:03:37.292
The final test remains reserved.

00:03:38.422 --> 00:03:43.292
Seventy two source checks and independent
audits reproduce the saved predictions,

00:03:43.602 --> 00:03:44.072
objectives,

00:03:44.442 --> 00:03:45.752
identities and metrics.

00:03:46.362 --> 00:03:50.452
They do not supply a learner
explanation or hosted recovery proof.

00:03:51.002 --> 00:03:55.192
The next review is to distinguish
loss influence, weight preference,

00:03:55.582 --> 00:03:58.472
coordinate conditioning and
representational capacity.

00:03:59.042 --> 00:04:01.952
That understanding is the
purpose of these implementations,

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:06.372
even when the established baseline
remains the model we carry forward.

